--TEST--
An `exit($n)` puts the very `$n` on the report rather than a normalized 0 or 1, and that non-zero status arrives with `error` still `null` — `rule:observability/three-endings-fire-the-exit-queue`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Script\ExitReport::status
// covers: Core\Script\ExitReport::reason
// covers: Core\Script\ExitReport::error
// § 2's middle row, in the column no landed case reads: `exit($n)` is `status`
// `$n`. The two `exit` cases already on disk both end at 0, which is also the
// `Normal` row's status — so nothing yet tells "the report carries the number
// the program chose" from "the report carries 0 unless something threw".
//
// The number is chosen for what it is not. 42 is not 0, not 1, and not either
// of the two the other rows of § 2 impose, so a report that had normalized the
// status to a success/failure bit would print plausibly on any other ending and
// fails here.
int $chosen = 42;

// One number, read at the call site and at the report, so the assertion is that
// the two *agree* rather than that the report said 42. Capture is by value at
// the anonymous function (`rule:types/implicit-capture`) and `$chosen` is never rebound, so what the hook
// holds is what the `exit` below is handed.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (Core\Script\ExitReport $report): void => {
    string $named = match ($report->reason()) {
        Core\Script\ExitReason::Normal => "Normal",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::ExitCall => "ExitCall",
        Core\Script\ExitReason::UncaughtThrow => "UncaughtThrow",
    };
    ?Throwable $error = $report->error();
    echo "exit hook: ", $named, " ", $report->status(), " ",
        $error == null ? "no error" : "an error", "\n";

    if ($report->status() == $chosen) {
        echo "the status is the number the call named\n";
    } else {
        echo "the status is not the number the call named\n";
    }

    // The other half, and the reason the two columns are asserted together: a
    // status of 1 is what `script-on-exit-carries-the-live-throwable-of-an-
    // uncaught-throw.nvst` pins for a throw, so "not 0" reads like "something
    // threw" until a case names an ending that is both. `reason` and `error`
    // are the columns that tell § 2's rows apart; `status` never was one.
    if ($report->status() != 0 && $error == null) {
        echo "a failing status is not evidence of a throw\n";
    }
});

echo "before the exit\n";
exit($chosen);
--EXPECT--
before the exit
exit hook: ExitCall 42 no error
the status is the number the call named
a failing status is not evidence of a throw
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
%A
